※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none host=host.c
※ pl4g-test: args -shared

※ `constinit` in a shared object: the initializers give the variables their
※ values when the object is loaded, before its constructors, and what the
※ object offers reads them -- under the same rule the checker holds an
※ executable to.
※
※ **The loader runs what an object's `.init_array` lists, after it has acted
※ on `PT_GNU_RELRO`**, so the pages that header names are read-only before
※ any of it runs, and what the initializers write cannot be there.  The
※ variables stay in a section of their own, `.pl4g.relro`, which is writable
※ data of the object and not in that header: nothing makes them read-only
※ once the initializers have run.  Doing it would take a function of the
※ object's own run last and a segment of its own for the pages, in a process
※ whose other code -- the host's, written in anything -- may write anything
※ anyway; what keeps the object's own code from writing them is the checker,
※ which is the same as in an executable.  The initializers are listed first,
※ the constructors after them, so a constructor reads them given.

let base: i64 = 2i64

fn ten(from: &static i64) → i64:
    from⌖ × 5i64

constinit let chosen: &static i64 = pick()

constinit let scale: i64 = ten(chosen) × 2i64

fn pick() → &static i64:
    &base

let started: mut i64 = 0i64

@[constructor, impure]
fn begin():
    started ← scale

@[visible, cdecl]
fn run(n: i64) → i64:
    n × scale + chosen⌖ + started - 20i64
